<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sixth Dynasty of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2345–2181 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/24th-century-bce">24th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sixth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was the last major dynasty of the Old Kingdom, with kings including Teti, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepi-i">Pepi I</a>, Merenre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepi-ii">Pepi II</a>, whose reign was among the longest in history. Its officials led expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, and objects of Pepi I were dedicated in the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>. Growing power of provincial governors marked its last decades, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sixth-dynasty-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Sixth Dynasty of Egypt" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Keith Schengili-Roberts · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AnkhnesmeryreII-and-Son-PepiII-SideView_BrooklynMuseum.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
